// Original SPARK teaching notes, mapped to the supplied CXC 27/O/SYLL 24.
// [section, objective numbers, title, teaching paragraphs, worked case, common mistake, check, answer]
export const OA_LESSONS = [
[1,[1,2],"What the office does and how a business is organised",[
`An office is the information and coordination centre of an organisation. It receives facts, checks and processes them, stores records and sends useful information to the people who need it. A customer order starts as data: a name, product code and quantity. After checking stock and price, the office produces information that the warehouse can act on. The office also supports production, distribution, buying, selling, payments and the control of the business. It is a function, so it can operate in a building, from home or across several locations.`,
`The business's purpose and goals influence its office. A small plumbing firm may have one clerk answering calls, booking jobs and preparing invoices. A large hotel may separate reception, purchasing, human resources and accounts. Centralisation puts a function under one common team; decentralisation places responsibility closer to individual branches or departments. Centralisation can reduce duplication and improve consistency; decentralisation can make local responses faster. Neither arrangement is automatically best.`,
`An organisation chart shows positions and their relationships. Read a solid vertical line as a reporting relationship, not as a friendship. Authority is the right to direct work; responsibility is the duty to do it; accountability means being answerable for the result. A clerk should know whom to ask for approval and where to refer an exception. Clear lines of communication prevent two departments from giving conflicting instructions.`
],`At a Kingston furniture company, the sales clerk records an order, the stores clerk confirms availability, dispatch schedules delivery and accounts issues an invoice. If stores reports that an item is unavailable, sales must agree a new arrangement with the customer before dispatch promises a date. Trace the information through all four departments: a delay in one office affects the whole service.`,
`Do not describe an office as only a room containing desks. Explain the work it does and how that work supports the organisation. An organisation chart shows posts; it does not show every informal conversation.`,
`Why should a sales clerk check stock before confirming delivery?`,
`The stock record turns a customer's request into a workable order. Checking prevents an unavailable item being promised and lets sales arrange an alternative before dispatch plans the delivery.`],
[1,[3,4],"Office layouts, ergonomics and safe working",[
`A cellular office uses separate rooms. It offers privacy and fewer interruptions, which may suit confidential interviews, but can cost more and slow communication. An open-plan office puts several workers in a shared space. It can improve supervision and teamwork, but noise and lack of privacy need control. A landscaped layout uses planned work areas, furniture and screens to organise an open space. A virtual office coordinates work through communications technology without requiring everyone to occupy one permanent office.`,
`Judge a layout against the task. Reception should be easy to find without giving visitors unrestricted access to staff or records. Keep frequently used equipment within convenient reach and arrange a sensible route from incoming work to completed work. A clear aisle is not spare storage space. Consider lighting, ventilation, temperature, accessibility, noise, cable management and emergency exits as well as appearance.`,
`Ergonomics means fitting the work and equipment to the person. Adjust a chair so the back is supported and feet rest securely; position the keyboard and mouse so shoulders and wrists are relaxed. Put the screen where it can be read without bending the neck or fighting glare. Alternate tasks and take appropriate movement breaks. Report damaged electrical equipment, clean up spills promptly and follow the organisation's fire, first-aid and evacuation procedures.`
],`A receptionist works beside a bright window and twists repeatedly to reach a shared printer. Move or shade the screen to reduce glare, position frequently used items within easy reach and rearrange the workstation so routine tasks do not require repeated twisting. The aim is to reduce strain while keeping the visitor route clear.`,
`Do not recommend an expensive open-plan office simply because it looks modern. Link the choice to privacy, communication, cost and the actual work. Ergonomics includes the task and environment, not just buying an adjustable chair.`,
`Give one reason for using a private room for a personnel interview.`,
`A private room protects confidential discussion and reduces interruptions. The reason must relate to the work being done.`],
[1,[5,6],"Choosing and caring for office resources",[
`Office resources include people, information, money, furniture, stationery, equipment, software, time and space. They work together. A scanner does not improve a records system unless someone scans accurately, names files consistently and stores them securely. A printer with a low purchase price can still be costly if ink, repairs and wasted paper are expensive.`,
`Begin a purchasing decision with the need: what work must be done, how often, by whom and to what standard? Compare capacity, reliability, compatibility, running costs, maintenance, warranty, supplier support, ease of use, accessibility and environmental impact. Consider whether to buy, rent, lease or outsource. A high-volume office may justify a multifunction machine; a small office may use a simpler printer and a secure external service for occasional specialist work.`,
`Care for resources through authorised use, staff training, scheduled maintenance, accurate stock records and sensible energy use. Keep liquids away from equipment and do not attempt an electrical repair without authority and competence. Protect information through controlled access, backups and secure disposal. Reducing waste should not mean reusing paper that exposes confidential information.`
],`Two printers both cost $45,000. Printer A costs $4 per page and Printer B costs $2.50 per page. At 8,000 pages, the running costs are $32,000 and $20,000. Before choosing B, also check its speed, warranty, network compatibility and the availability of consumables. The cheaper operating cost is useful evidence, not the only decision factor.`,
`The cheapest purchase is not always the lowest total cost. Do not confuse a resource's contribution with a list of brand names or assume that every task requires new technology.`,
`Why should an office check software compatibility before purchasing a new scanner?`,
`The scanner must work with the existing computers, file formats and records software. Incompatible equipment can create extra cost or prevent the intended work from being completed.`],
[1,[7,8],"Professional behaviour and good working relationships",[
`Employers need technical skills and dependable conduct. Office staff should communicate clearly, handle numbers and documents accurately, organise time, use relevant technology and solve routine problems. Desirable attitudes include courtesy, initiative, willingness to learn, punctuality, honesty, discretion and respect for others. Initiative means taking a sensible action within your authority; it does not mean changing an approved procedure without permission.`,
`Good human relationships make work easier to coordinate. Listen without interrupting, clarify requests, share information with authorised colleagues and give constructive feedback about the work rather than attacking a person. Respect cultural differences and accessibility needs. When duties conflict, explain the problem to the appropriate supervisor and agree priorities instead of silently missing a deadline.`,
`Confidentiality is especially important around customers, pay records, appointments and business plans. Discuss sensitive matters only with people entitled to know. Social responsibility includes considerate use of resources and fair treatment of customers and colleagues. A professional social-media presence also matters: do not post workplace photographs, customer details or internal complaints without authorisation.`
],`An accounts clerk needs a signed delivery record before paying a supplier, but dispatch has not returned it. A useful message identifies the order, explains the deadline and asks when the record can be supplied. Blaming dispatch in a group chat creates friction without solving the problem. Agree an action, record it and follow up courteously.`,
`Being friendly does not justify sharing private information. Being assertive means expressing a need clearly and respectfully; aggression and personal criticism usually damage cooperation.`,
`How can a clerk show initiative without exceeding authority?`,
`Identify a routine problem, gather the relevant facts and take an approved action or propose a solution to the supervisor. Obtain approval before making commitments outside the clerk's authority.`],
[2,[1,2],"Reception: the organisation's first point of contact",[
`Reception connects the visitor with the organisation. A courteous, organised welcome can build confidence before a customer meets anyone else. Poor handling can lead to lost appointments, security problems and complaints. Reception staff therefore support both customer goodwill and the efficient movement of information.`,
`A receptionist greets visitors, establishes the purpose of a visit, checks appointments, informs the host and directs or accompanies visitors according to procedure. Other duties can include operating the telephone system, taking messages, arranging appointments, handling enquiries and keeping the reception area orderly. Ask questions tactfully and collect only the information needed for the task.`,
`Follow the visitor-control procedure: verify the visitor's identity and appointment, contact the host, issue a pass if required and record arrival and departure. An appointment does not give a visitor permission to wander through the office. If a host is delayed, explain politely, offer an appropriate waiting arrangement and seek guidance. Be calm with an upset visitor, listen and refer matters beyond your authority. Do not promise an outcome simply to end the conversation.`
],`A customer arrives early for a 10 a.m. appointment with Ms James. Welcome the customer, confirm the name and appointment, notify Ms James and explain where to wait. If another person asks whom the customer is seeing, follow confidentiality procedures rather than announcing the purpose of the visit across the room.`,
`Do not treat reception as only answering the telephone. Courtesy, security, accurate information and orderly visitor handling all contribute to the organisation's welfare.`,
`What should a receptionist do when a visitor arrives without an appointment?`,
`Establish the visitor's identity and purpose, contact the appropriate employee and follow the visitor procedure. Do not send the visitor directly into restricted areas or guarantee that the employee will be available.`],
[2,[3],"Telephone calls and useful written messages",[
`Answer promptly using the organisation's approved greeting, identify the organisation and yourself where required, speak clearly and listen. Keep a professional tone even when a caller is impatient. Before transferring a call, establish whom the caller needs and explain the transfer. Ask permission before putting someone on hold, return regularly and offer an alternative when the wait will be long.`,
`A message should normally include the date and time, caller's name and organisation, intended recipient, contact details, the substance of the call, the action requested and the name of the person taking the message. Write down names, numbers, dates and references carefully. Read important details back to the caller: a single wrong digit can make the whole message unusable.`,
`Distinguish an exact request from your own interpretation. If the caller asks for an update on order 417 before 3 p.m., do not write only 'called about an order' and do not promise delivery. Mark urgency accurately, relay the message promptly through the approved channel and protect confidential information. For an outgoing call, prepare the number, purpose, documents and questions before dialling; identify yourself and record any agreed action.`
],`Denise Davis calls for Mr Brown about purchase order 417 and requests a return call on 876-555-0147 before 3 p.m. A useful message preserves all five details: caller, recipient, reference, callback number and requested time. Read the number back and check whether 'before 3 p.m.' is today before ending the call.`,
`Do not substitute your own promise for a caller's request. Writing a neat message is not enough if its number or deadline is wrong. Personal calls and background conversation should not interfere with service.`,
`Why is 'Please call Mr Brown' an inadequate message when several people called?`,
`It does not identify the caller, contact details, purpose, time or action needed. A useful message lets the recipient return the correct call and deal with the actual request.`],
[2,[4,5],"Appointment diaries and reminder systems",[
`An appointment diary records agreed meetings and protects the executive's time. Establish the purpose, people involved, preferred date, expected length, location or meeting link and any preparation required. Check existing commitments, travel time, opening hours and the executive's instructions before offering a slot. Confirm the agreed details with everyone concerned.`,
`A paper diary is quick to consult and does not depend on a device, but changes must be recorded clearly and securely. An electronic calendar can share availability, send invitations and reminders and help detect clashes, but access permissions, connectivity and time-zone settings must be correct. Avoid keeping two conflicting diaries without a clear procedure for synchronising them.`,
`A reminder or tickler system brings work back to attention on the date action is needed. It may use dated paper folders, a diary entry or an electronic task notification. Set a reminder early enough to prepare, not merely at the final deadline. When an appointment changes, amend the original entry, notify the people affected, update associated arrangements and confirm the replacement. Never silently delete a commitment that another person still expects to attend.`
],`A manager has a meeting from 9:00 to 9:45 and needs 20 minutes to travel to a customer. A new appointment at 10:00 is unsuitable because only 15 minutes are available. Offer a later slot or change another arrangement with approval. Add a preparation reminder for the documents needed before departure.`,
`A diary entry records an appointment; a reminder prompts action. Do not assume that a calendar invitation has been accepted or ignore travel and preparation time because the meeting itself fits in the gap.`,
`What should be updated when an executive reschedules a meeting?`,
`Update the diary and reminders, notify participants, confirm the new details and amend rooms, travel, equipment and documents associated with the original appointment.`],[3,[1,2,3],"Choose a communication channel that fits the message",[
`Communication is successful when the receiver understands the intended message and can respond appropriately. Internal communication stays within the organisation; external communication connects it with customers, suppliers and the public. Downward communication gives instructions or information from a higher level, upward communication carries reports or feedback to management, and horizontal communication coordinates colleagues at a similar level. Oral, written, visual and electronic methods can serve any of these routes.`,
`Choose a method by considering urgency, confidentiality, cost, distance, available technology, the receiver's needs and whether a permanent record is required. A telephone call is useful for an urgent clarification, but a written confirmation is better for preserving an agreed specification. An email can reach several authorised people quickly; it can also be overlooked, sent to the wrong address or forwarded without care. A meeting is useful when people need to discuss and decide, but unnecessary meetings consume working time.`,
`Barriers include noise, jargon, language differences, a poor connection, incomplete information, the wrong channel, assumptions, unsuitable timing and emotional reactions. Reduce them by using plain language, choosing an appropriate time and place, checking facts and inviting feedback. A nod or a sent-email notification does not prove understanding. Ask the receiver to confirm a crucial date, quantity or next action in their own words.`
],`A supplier needs to know today whether to substitute blue folders for green ones. Call the authorised purchasing officer to resolve the urgent choice, then send the approved details in writing. A public social-media comment would be unsuitable because it exposes business information and may not reach the decision-maker.`,
`Do not say that email is always the best method. Explain why a method suits this message and this receiver. Sending information is not the same as confirming that it was understood.`,
`Why might an urgent telephone agreement need a written follow-up?`,
`The call settles the issue quickly; the written follow-up preserves the exact agreement, responsibilities and deadline for both parties.`],
[3,[4,5],"Stationery and reliable sources of information",[
`Stationery should suit the document and the way it will be handled. Letterheads identify the organisation; continuation sheets carry later pages; forms collect information in a consistent order; envelopes protect and address the contents. Consider paper size, weight, number of copies, printing method, storage, postage and confidentiality. Use the approved stationery and keep quantities under control.`,
`A4 paper is commonly used for letters and reports; A5 can suit smaller notices or forms, while A3 provides more space for large charts. Select an envelope that accommodates the contents appropriately: a document that must remain flat needs a different choice from an ordinary folded letter. Window envelopes require the address to align correctly. Check the letter and enclosures before sealing; an attractive letterhead cannot correct a missing attachment.`,
`Information sources include internal files, directories, manuals, official websites, reference books, databases, schedules, trade publications and people with relevant responsibility. Decide what fact is needed before searching. Check who produced the source, its date, accuracy, relevance and authority. A current supplier price list is useful for a quotation; an old invoice may show a historical price rather than the amount available today. Record the source when the information will be used in a report.`
],`A clerk prepares a board pack containing a letter and a large unfolded chart. Check the chart dimensions and the requirement to keep it flat before selecting the envelope. Obtain the current meeting date from the approved notice rather than reusing the date on an earlier pack. Match each material and source to its purpose.`,
`Do not treat every search result as authoritative or assume that the largest paper size is always best. The question asks you to select and justify an appropriate resource.`,
`Which is the better source for today's delivery charge: an old customer invoice or the carrier's current published schedule?`,
`Use the carrier's current schedule, checking the destination, weight and service. The old invoice describes a previous transaction and may no longer apply.`],
[3,[6],"Telecommunications and virtual office services",[
`Telecommunication services allow offices to exchange voice, documents, images and data across distance. Examples include fixed and mobile telephone services, internet access, messaging, voice mail, video conferencing and data services. A virtual meeting can bring colleagues from different islands together without requiring travel, but its usefulness depends on preparation and a reliable connection.`,
`Compare services against the work. Consider coverage, reliability, bandwidth, speed, cost, technical support, compatibility, security and the needs of users with disabilities. A package that is cheap but unreliable during customer-service hours can damage the business. Cloud services can support shared documents and remote access, but permissions and an agreed version-control procedure remain necessary.`,
`For a conference call or online meeting, check the invitation, connection, microphone, camera and necessary files in advance. Use the correct link and access settings, identify participants and follow meeting etiquette. Share only the intended screen or window. Protect passwords and verify requests for confidential information through an approved route. Have a sensible fallback, such as an authorised telephone number, if the connection fails.`
],`A Tobago office and a Kingston branch need to review a stock spreadsheet. A video meeting with controlled screen sharing lets them discuss the same figures. The organiser circulates the correct version first, restricts the meeting invitation to participants and arranges a telephone fallback. A faster internet plan alone would not solve an incorrect spreadsheet or an exposed meeting link.`,
`Do not confuse a provider's service with a device: a smartphone is equipment, while mobile voice or data access is a service. Technology does not remove the need to verify recipients and protect records.`,
`Why should a clerk test screen sharing before a virtual meeting?`,
`It reduces delays and helps ensure that only the intended material is displayed, protecting both meeting time and confidential information.`],
[3,[7,8,9],"Receive, sort and dispatch mail and parcels",[
`Incoming mail may arrive by post, courier, hand delivery or electronic means. Follow the office procedure for recording receipt, checking addresses, identifying urgent items, opening authorised mail, date-stamping and sorting it, noting enclosures and routing it promptly. Mail marked personal or confidential should go to the authorised recipient unopened unless a specific approved procedure says otherwise. Treat suspicious packages under the organisation's safety instructions rather than opening them to investigate.`,
`Outgoing mail should be checked for the correct addressee, date, signature, enclosures, attachments and return details. Choose a dispatch service by urgency, cost, destination, weight, value, tracking and proof-of-delivery requirements. Ordinary post may suit a routine letter; a tracked or registered service may suit an important original document. A courier can offer speed and collection arrangements. The exact services and conditions should be checked with the provider.`,
`For parcels, pack the contents securely, use cushioning where needed, label the destination and sender clearly and complete the required dispatch or customs information. Record the reference and retain the receipt. On receipt, check the package and accompanying documents, record discrepancies and obtain an acknowledgement where required. A large office may have a mailroom and collection schedule; a small office may combine these duties, but accuracy, custody and confidentiality are still necessary.`
],`An office sends an original signed agreement to a supplier. Check the signature and enclosures, confirm the address, choose a service with suitable tracking or proof of delivery and record the tracking reference. If an incoming letter says that three certificates are enclosed but only two are present, record the discrepancy and report it before filing the letter as complete.`,
`Do not put an unopened confidential letter through the ordinary opening routine. A dispatch book records what was sent; it does not by itself prove that the addressee received it.`,
`Why should the clerk retain a parcel's tracking number?`,
`It links the dispatch record to the carrier's service and helps trace the parcel, answer enquiries and investigate a delay or delivery dispute.`],
[4,[1,4],"Meeting terms and the different kinds of meetings",[
`A meeting brings people together to exchange information, consult or make decisions. Formal meetings follow established rules and documents; informal meetings are less structured. An Annual General Meeting is held periodically for the organisation's required annual business; an extraordinary or special meeting deals with business that cannot wait for the usual cycle. Committee, departmental and staff meetings each serve a different group and purpose. Virtual and face-to-face describe how people attend, not whether the business is formal.`,
`A notice announces a meeting. The agenda lists its business in the intended order. A quorum is the minimum attendance required under the applicable rules before valid business can be conducted. A motion is a proposal put to the meeting; an amendment proposes a change to it. A resolution is a decision formally adopted. Minutes are the written record of proceedings and decisions. Adjournment pauses or ends the sitting under its rules; it does not erase decisions already made.`,
`Read a meeting scenario by asking who must attend, what must be decided, which rules apply and what records are required. A chairperson keeps the discussion orderly and fair, while members contribute relevant information, consider proposals and vote where entitled. Do not assume that every organisation has the same quorum, voting rights or notice period.`
],`A club's rules require eight eligible members for a quorum. Only six attend its general meeting. The chairperson must apply the club's rules for that situation rather than declaring a binding vote merely because everyone present agrees. An informal discussion may still be useful, but it is not a substitute for validly conducting the required formal business.`,
`Do not confuse an agenda with minutes or a motion with an adopted resolution. A video meeting can be formal and still require a quorum and an accurate record.`,
`What is the difference between a motion and a resolution?`,
`A motion is a proposal for consideration. Once a proposal is adopted through the required procedure, the resulting decision is recorded as a resolution.`],
[4,[2,3],"Prepare meeting documents and record decisions",[
`A meeting notice identifies the organisation, meeting type, date, time, venue or access link and the authorised person issuing it. The agenda should list business in a clear sequence. A typical formal sequence includes apologies, confirmation of previous minutes, matters arising, reports, new business and arrangements for the next meeting, but the organisation's own rules take precedence. Circulate documents early enough for members to prepare.`,
`The secretary arranges notices and papers, checks attendance records, takes notes and prepares draft minutes. The chairperson manages the meeting, keeps the discussion relevant, puts proposals to the meeting where required and confirms decisions. Members prepare, contribute, observe the rules and carry out agreed tasks. These roles support each other; the secretary should not invent a decision because it seems a sensible conclusion.`,
`Minutes normally identify the meeting, date, place, those present, apologies and the business conducted. Record decisions, motions and voting outcomes where necessary, with responsibility and deadlines for action. Use clear, concise, accurate language. Minutes are not usually a word-for-word transcript or a record of the secretary's opinion. Follow the procedure for checking, approval, signature, distribution and secure storage.`
],`The committee agrees that Ms Thomas will obtain three catering quotations by 8 May. A useful minute records that decision, Ms Thomas's responsibility and the deadline. 'Catering discussed' is too vague to guide action. If the committee only considers a suggestion without adopting it, do not record it as an instruction to purchase.`,
`Do not write minutes as a personal account of who sounded convincing. Separate an actual decision from discussion and leave an uncertain fact to be checked rather than guessing.`,
`What makes an action point useful after a meeting?`,
`It identifies the agreed task, the person responsible and the deadline, so progress can be followed up and reported.`],
[4,[5,6,7],"Organise a meeting and follow up its business",[
`Start with the meeting's purpose and the people whose attendance is needed. Agree a suitable date, time and location; check room capacity, access, seating, equipment, refreshments and likely costs. For a virtual meeting, arrange the platform, link, access controls and technical support. Provide documents and accessibility arrangements in advance and tell participants how to report a problem.`,
`An AGM must follow the organisation's governing rules and applicable requirements. Check the required notice, eligible participants, quorum, voting procedures, reports, accounts, election arrangements and records. The legal detail can differ by organisation and jurisdiction, so a clerk should use the approved rules and instructions instead of inventing a universal notice period or quorum.`,
`After the meeting, prepare the draft minutes promptly, seek the required checking or approval and circulate the authorised record. Extract action points into a follow-up list with owners and due dates. Send reminders, obtain progress updates and place unresolved matters on the next appropriate agenda. Process authorised expenses, return equipment, file documents securely and update the meeting calendar.`
],`A branch meeting decides to improve a delivery procedure. The secretary records three actions, assigns an owner to each and sets review dates. Before the next meeting, the secretary asks for progress and circulates the approved information. Repeating the same unresolved item at every meeting without assigning responsibility is not effective follow-up.`,
`Booking a room is only one part of organising a meeting. A meeting is not complete when participants leave: the decisions need an accurate record and planned action.`,
`Why should an organiser check the governing rules before issuing an AGM notice?`,
`The rules determine required notice, eligible participation and valid meeting procedures. Using an ordinary staff-meeting arrangement could make the AGM's business improperly conducted.`],
[5,[1,3],"Plan business travel from the purpose of the visit",[
`Begin with the traveller's purpose, destination, required arrival date, appointments, budget and preferences allowed by policy. Confirm who authorises travel and spending. Check suitable transport, routes, accommodation, local transfers, communication needs and the time required between each part of the journey. A low fare is not a saving if the traveller arrives after the meeting.`,
`Obtain information from reliable current sources such as the carrier, accommodation provider, official entry-information service or approved travel agent. Check booking conditions, baggage, cancellation terms, connections, accessibility and emergency contact arrangements. Keep records of approvals and confirmations. When information changes, notify the traveller and amend the itinerary and related appointments.`,
`An itinerary places the journey in chronological order. Include relevant dates, departure and arrival locations, local times, flight or service references, accommodation details, appointments, transfers and useful contacts. Distinguish a reservation from a confirmed booking and identify any action still required. Share the itinerary with authorised people and avoid exposing personal documents in a broadly accessible calendar.`
],`An executive has a 10 a.m. meeting in another island. Compare an early arrival with a flight landing at 9:30. Landing time is not the time the traveller reaches the meeting: allow for arrival procedures, baggage and transfer. If the schedule is unrealistic, propose an earlier flight or travel the previous day within the approved budget.`,
`Do not plan from departure time alone or treat a booking request as confirmation. Verify each connection and leave realistic time for transfers and preparation.`,
`Why should an itinerary include local transfers?`,
`The journey does not end at the airport or terminal. Transfer arrangements and travel time affect whether the traveller can reach the accommodation or appointment on time.`],
[5,[2],"Travel documents and what each document proves",[
`A passport establishes identity and nationality for international travel. A visa, where required, indicates permission under the destination's rules; it is not the same document as a passport or a transport ticket. A ticket or electronic booking record identifies the travel service, while a boarding pass is issued for boarding a particular service. Check the information supplied by the relevant authority and carrier for the actual journey.`,
`Other relevant documents may include travel insurance, health documentation, an itinerary, accommodation confirmation, an authorisation letter and documents for carrying business equipment or samples. Travel insurance can cover specified risks under its conditions; it is not a guarantee that every loss will be paid. Keep important reference numbers and emergency contacts accessible while protecting the originals and sensitive personal details.`,
`Before travel, verify names against identity documents and check validity, dates, destinations and booking details. Make secure copies where policy allows and explain where the traveller can access them. Entry and health requirements can change; use current official information instead of relying on a colleague's experience from a previous year.`
],`A reservation is made under 'M. Brown', but the traveller's official identity document shows a different full name. Ask the carrier or booking service to confirm the required correction before departure. Do not assume that an itinerary proves identity or that a valid passport removes every other entry requirement.`,
`A visa is not a ticket, a ticket is not a boarding pass and a hotel booking is not proof of permission to enter a country. State the purpose of each document clearly.`,
`Why should the clerk check the traveller's name before confirming a booking?`,
`The booking details need to match the identification required by the carrier and relevant authorities. An error can cause delays, extra charges or difficulty travelling.`],
[5,[4,5],"Time zones, travel money and a workable schedule",[
`Places can use different local times. Read the time zone for each departure, arrival and appointment, and take account of the date as well as the hour. If a destination is one hour ahead, 10 a.m. at the origin is 11 a.m. there. A flight duration cannot be calculated reliably by subtracting two clock readings from different zones without first putting them on a common time basis.`,
`Use the offset supplied in an examination question. For real bookings, verify the current local time and any seasonal clock changes. When scheduling a virtual meeting, communicate the date and the time in each participant's location or specify an unambiguous time zone. Check whether a journey crosses midnight and changes the arrival date.`,
`Travel payments may use cash, debit or credit cards, bank transfers, prepaid cards and other services accepted at the destination. Compare acceptance, access to funds, charges, exchange rate, security, proof of payment and the organisation's policy. A quoted exchange rate needs a clear direction: at US$1 = J$150, US$80 is J$12,000 before any stated fee. Keep receipts and reconcile an authorised travel advance after the visit.`
],`A flight leaves at 08:00 from a place using UTC−5 and takes three hours. The destination uses UTC−4. Departure is 13:00 UTC; arrival is 16:00 UTC, or 12:00 at the destination. Use the given offsets in this exercise rather than assuming the aircraft arrives at 11:00 local time.`,
`Do not ignore the arrival date or reverse the exchange-rate calculation. Distinguish the rate quoted in a question from a current market rate, which must be checked for a real transaction.`,
`At US$1 = J$150, how many Jamaican dollars are needed for US$40 before fees?`,
`Multiply 40 by 150: J$6,000. Any separately stated conversion charge must then be included according to the question.`],
[6,[1,2],"Records that can be found, trusted and protected",[
`A record is evidence of a transaction, decision or activity. It may be a letter, invoice, employee record, email, image or database entry. Records management controls the record from creation and use through storage, retention and authorised disposal. The records clerk receives and classifies material, indexes and files it, retrieves it when authorised, tracks its movement and helps protect it from loss or unauthorised access.`,
`A useful system is accurate, accessible to authorised users, economical, secure, sufficiently flexible and simple enough for staff to follow consistently. Accessibility does not mean that everyone can open every file. The right person must be able to locate the right version within a reasonable time. A fast search of badly named, inaccurate files is still a poor records system.`,
`Agree naming conventions, responsibilities, access levels and a procedure for checking completeness. Record key facts consistently: a customer number should not change spelling from one document to the next. Keep an audit trail where required so the organisation can establish who accessed or changed a record. Protect physical files against fire, water and theft, and electronic records against unauthorised access, alteration and loss.`
],`A customer asks why an order was delayed. The clerk retrieves the original order, the approved change and the dispatch record using the same order reference. These records let the business establish what was requested and agreed. A folder containing only the latest unsourced email would not provide the same evidence.`,
`Do not confuse records management with simply putting old papers in cupboards. A reliable system also controls creation, access, versions, movement and disposal.`,
`Why should a file reference be used consistently across related documents?`,
`A consistent reference links the documents to the same matter and makes accurate retrieval, checking and follow-up easier.`],
[6,[3,4],"Classify, index, file and retrieve records",[
`Alphabetical filing arranges indexed names in letter order. Compare the surname first for personal names, then the given names if surnames match. Numerical filing arranges assigned numbers and normally needs an index linking names to numbers. Subject filing groups records by topic, geographical filing by location and chronological filing by date. Alphanumeric systems combine letters and numbers. Choose according to the type of information and how staff will search for it.`,
`Before filing, check that the document has been dealt with and is authorised for filing, classify its subject or owner, select the correct index heading and reference, and put it in the right location. Cross-reference when users may reasonably search under another heading. Follow a documented rule for company names, prefixes and unusual names rather than making a different choice each time.`,
`When a paper file is removed, use the approved out-card, tracer or movement register to record who has it, where it has gone and when it should return. For electronic records, use agreed folder structures, metadata and version control. Search results need checking: two files with similar names may relate to different customers. Return material promptly and keep the filing area orderly.`
],`Six records are indexed Brown, Marcia; Brown, Mark; Brown, Michael; Browne, Martin; Campbell, Alan; Clarke, Denise. Compare surnames letter by letter: Brown precedes Browne. For the three Brown records, Marcia precedes Mark, which precedes Michael. Use the filing practice to work through the order rather than relying on the first letter alone.`,
`A numerical system is not automatically searchable by customer name; it needs an index. Do not remove a paper file without a movement record or silently overwrite an electronic version needed as evidence.`,
`Why is an out-card left when a file is borrowed?`,
`It shows that the file is temporarily elsewhere and records the person or location to contact, reducing lost files and unnecessary searching.`],
[6,[5,6],"Inactive records, retention, access and secure disposal",[
`An inactive file is no longer needed frequently for current work. It may still be required for reference, legal obligations or historical value. Review records under an authorised retention schedule: keep them in active storage while in frequent use, transfer or archive them when appropriate, and dispose of them only when authorised. Age alone is not sufficient reason to destroy a file.`,
`Retention and access requirements vary by record, organisation and jurisdiction. A clerk should use the applicable policy and instructions, especially for employee, financial, health, customer and legal records. A record connected with an unresolved dispute or an official preservation instruction must not be destroyed merely because a routine retention date has arrived. Seek guidance rather than guessing a retention period.`,
`Protect personal information by collecting only what is needed, using it for authorised purposes, limiting access, keeping it accurate and arranging secure disposal. Paper records may need shredding or another approved destruction service; electronic records may require secure deletion or media sanitisation rather than merely moving a file to the recycle bin. Keep the required disposal authorisation and record. An archive is controlled storage, not an unattended pile of unwanted boxes.`
],`A former employee's file has reached the routine review date, but a matter involving that employment is still unresolved. Refer the file for guidance and preserve it under the applicable instruction. Do not shred it automatically. When records are eventually authorised for disposal, protect the contents during transport and destruction as well as while they are in the office.`,
`Inactive does not mean worthless. Deleting a shortcut does not delete the underlying electronic record, and an ordinary rubbish bin is unsuitable for confidential documents.`,
`What should happen before an inactive file is destroyed?`,
`Check the retention schedule, any outstanding preservation requirement and the required authorisation. Use an approved secure disposal method and retain the disposal record where required.`],
[6,[7,8],"Records equipment and centralised or departmental systems",[
`Manual resources include filing cabinets, shelves, folders, guides, indexes, labels, out-cards and secure storage. Electronic resources include computers, scanners, document-management software, databases, storage devices and backup systems. Select resources for the volume, format, security and retrieval needs of the records. Scanning creates an electronic copy; it does not automatically verify the text or authorise disposal of the original.`,
`Centralised filing places records under one common service. It can improve standardisation, specialist control and use of space, but staff may need to wait for retrieval and access across locations must be planned. Decentralised filing keeps records in the departments that use them. It can speed local access and suit specialist work, but may duplicate resources and produce inconsistent procedures. A mixed system can centralise some records while leaving active departmental records locally controlled.`,
`Electronic records need access controls, backups, recovery procedures, appropriate search fields and staff training. A backup is a recovery copy; it is not a substitute for an organised working file system. Test whether records can be restored. Apply the same confidentiality principles to shared drives, cloud services and remote working as to a locked filing cabinet.`
],`A hotel keeps routine supplier invoices in a central accounts records service but gives authorised HR staff separate control of personnel files. Both use an agreed retention and naming policy. This combines efficient common procedures with appropriate access restrictions rather than choosing a single arrangement for every kind of record.`,
`Do not assume that an electronic system is safe merely because it has a password or that centralisation gives every employee access to all records.`,
`Give one advantage and one risk of decentralised filing.`,
`Records can be retrieved quickly by the department using them, but different departments may duplicate records or apply inconsistent naming and retention procedures unless standards are coordinated.`],
[7,[1,2],"The human resources office and its confidential records",[
`Human Resource Management supports the people needed for the organisation's work. Its functions include workforce planning, recruitment and selection, induction, training and development, employee records, attendance, welfare, performance procedures and employee relations. Some activities are shared with line managers and payroll. HR should help the organisation meet its staffing needs while treating employees fairly and consistently.`,
`An HR clerk may organise applications and interviews, maintain authorised personnel records, prepare routine correspondence, record leave and attendance and help arrange training. Accuracy and discretion matter because errors can affect pay, leave, opportunities and trust. The clerk should be organised, courteous, impartial and able to handle sensitive information without discussing it casually.`,
`Follow a clear procedure when information changes. Verify the authority for the change, update the correct employee record, retain the necessary supporting evidence and notify the appropriate office through an approved channel. Do not promise a job, promotion or leave approval unless authorised. Direct questions about policy or personal entitlements to the responsible person.`
],`A supervisor sends an approved change to an employee's work schedule. The HR clerk confirms the employee reference and effective date, updates the authorised record and informs payroll if necessary. The clerk does not change the pay rate simply because the schedule changed; the pay instruction needs its own authority.`,
`An HR clerk supports the process but does not automatically have authority to make employment decisions. Confidentiality applies to unsuccessful applicants as well as current employees.`,
`Why must HR communicate an approved employment change accurately to payroll?`,
`Payroll needs the correct effective date and authorised details to calculate pay properly. An incomplete or late notification can produce an incorrect payment.`],
[7,[3,4],"Find and evaluate employment opportunities",[
`Vacancies can be advertised through an organisation's website, newspapers, recruitment agencies, employment services, professional associations, school or college careers services and suitable online job platforms. Personal contacts can alert someone to a vacancy, but the applicant should verify the employer and the application procedure. Be cautious about requests for fees or unnecessary personal information.`,
`Read the advertisement carefully. Separate essential requirements from desirable qualities and note the job title, main duties, qualification or experience requirements, location, working hours, closing date and method of application. Consider pay, benefits, travel costs, development opportunities, working conditions, accessibility and whether the role matches your interests and abilities. A high stated salary may not compensate for impractical hours or excessive travel costs.`,
`Research the organisation using reliable information. Tailor the application to the role and use evidence of relevant skills from school, work experience, volunteering or other responsibilities. Keep a record of applications, deadlines and responses. Apply through the verified channel and follow the requested document and file-naming instructions.`
],`Two entry-level positions have similar pay. One requires a costly two-hour commute and evening work; the other offers relevant training near home. Compare the full conditions and your circumstances instead of choosing from the salary line alone. In both cases, verify the advertisement on the employer's official channel before sending an application.`,
`Do not apply with the same unedited letter for every vacancy or ignore the closing date. Avoid inventing qualifications to meet a requirement.`,
`What should an applicant check before sending personal documents to an online vacancy?`,
`Verify the employer and recruitment channel, understand which documents are genuinely required and use the authorised application procedure.`],
[7,[5,6],"Prepare job applications and employment correspondence",[
`An application letter identifies the vacancy and source, explains the applicant's interest and gives relevant evidence of suitability. Use a professional business-letter layout, an appropriate salutation and closing and accurate contact details. A curriculum vitae or résumé summarises education, skills, experience and other relevant evidence in a clear order. An application form requests the employer's required information in a standard format; follow its instructions and complete each applicable field accurately.`,
`Connect claims to evidence. Instead of writing only 'I am organised', describe maintaining a club register, preparing a meeting agenda or meeting deadlines on a project. Proofread names, dates, contact details, spelling and attachments. Obtain permission before naming a referee and provide accurate details. Do not include sensitive information that is not required.`,
`Employment correspondence may include an acceptance, acknowledgement, request, transfer application, leave application, resignation or other routine communication. State the purpose clearly, refer to relevant dates and documents and explain the action requested. Keep a respectful tone even when declining an offer or resigning. Follow the applicable agreement and procedure rather than inventing a universal notice period.`
],`A vacancy asks for a letter, a résumé and the reference OA-17 in the email subject. Prepare the two documents, name the files clearly, check that both are attached and place OA-17 in the subject. A well-written letter sent without the requested reference or attachment may be harder to process correctly.`,
`Do not confuse an application letter with a résumé: they support each other but serve different purposes. Never fabricate experience or provide a referee's details without permission.`,
`How can a school leaver support a claim of good organisational skills?`,
`Give a relevant truthful example, such as organising club records, scheduling an event or coordinating project deadlines, and explain the responsibility carried out.`],
[7,[7,8],"Prepare for an interview and enter the workplace",[
`Prepare for an interview by researching the employer and role, reviewing your application and gathering truthful examples of relevant skills. Confirm the date, time, location or video link, route and required documents. Choose suitable professional dress and allow time for delays. For a virtual interview, check sound, connection, background and access to the correct link.`,
`Listen to each question, answer clearly and relate examples to the role. If a question is unclear, ask for clarification rather than answering a different one. Be honest about what you know and show willingness to learn. Prepare sensible questions about duties, training or the work. Keep your telephone from interrupting and thank the panel courteously.`,
`After appointment, induction introduces the employee to the organisation, people, duties, procedures, safety arrangements and expected conduct. Learn attendance and reporting rules, use of equipment, confidentiality, customer service, emergency procedures and the route for asking questions. Training develops knowledge and skills; evaluation helps identify progress and further support. Being new is a reason to clarify an instruction, not to ignore it.`
],`During an interview, an applicant is asked about handling a deadline. A useful answer describes the task, the actions taken and the result rather than merely saying 'I work well under pressure'. On starting work, the new employee checks how urgent tasks should be prioritised and who must approve changes.`,
`Do not memorise a false story or arrive without knowing the role. A confident answer can still be inaccurate; honesty and relevant evidence are more useful than exaggeration.`,
`What is the purpose of induction?`,
`It helps a new employee understand the organisation, duties, colleagues, procedures, safety requirements and expected conduct so they can begin working effectively.`],
[7,[9,10],"Employee welfare and labour turnover",[
`Workplace rules and employment legislation can protect areas such as health and safety, equality of opportunity, fair treatment, working conditions, pay-related requirements and agreed benefits. The detailed rights and procedures depend on the jurisdiction and employment arrangement. For an actual case, use the relevant current rules and authorised advice. In an examination, explain the benefit of the protection described rather than inventing legal figures.`,
`Welfare measures can improve well-being, attendance, morale and trust. Safe equipment, clear procedures, appropriate training, fair treatment and accessible ways to raise concerns help employees work effectively. These measures also benefit the organisation by reducing avoidable accidents, disruption and conflict. Welfare is not limited to occasional entertainment or gifts.`,
`Labour turnover is the movement of employees into and out of an organisation, often discussed as employees leaving and needing replacement. Causes can include poor working conditions, low morale, limited advancement, unsuitable recruitment, relocation, retirement or better opportunities elsewhere. Some turnover is expected, but high avoidable turnover can increase recruitment and training costs, interrupt service and remove experience. Use attendance, exit information and other evidence to investigate causes before proposing improvements.`
],`A company repeatedly loses new clerks after one month. Exit information points to unclear duties and little training. Improving induction, supervision and role clarity addresses the reported cause more directly than simply offering another recruitment advertisement. Check whether the improvement reduces early departures.`,
`Do not assume every departure proves poor management or that higher pay alone solves every turnover problem. Link the proposed action to evidence about the cause.`,
`Give two costs of high avoidable labour turnover.`,
`The organisation may incur repeated recruitment and training costs and lose experienced staff, causing disruption, lower efficiency or poorer customer service.`],
[8,[1,2,4],"Inside the accounts office",[
`The accounts office records and controls the organisation's money transactions. It helps prepare invoices, collect amounts due, pay approved bills, maintain financial records, process payroll and provide information for decisions. Accounts payable concerns amounts owed to suppliers; accounts receivable concerns amounts owed by customers. These are not the same as money already paid or received.`,
`An accounts clerk checks documents, enters accurate data, files supporting evidence, prepares routine statements or schedules and refers discrepancies. Important attributes include honesty, accuracy, confidentiality, numeracy and the ability to follow procedures. Separate authorisation, custody and recording responsibilities where the system requires it: the person preparing a payment should not simply approve their own unsupported request.`,
`Calculators, computers, spreadsheets, accounting software, scanners and secure payment systems can support the work. Use the correct formulas, account references, access rights and backup procedures. A spreadsheet produces an incorrect result if its input or formula is wrong. Reconcile totals and supporting documents rather than assuming a computer-generated figure is automatically reliable.`
],`A supplier invoice arrives for 20 cartridges, but the delivery record confirms only 18 accepted cartridges. The clerk checks the purchase order and receipt record and raises the discrepancy before authorising payment. Paying the full invoice without checking would weaken the control even if the amount was entered neatly into a spreadsheet.`,
`An invoice requests payment; a receipt acknowledges payment. Do not describe every accounts task as simply 'handling cash' or treat software as a substitute for authorised documents.`,
`Why should accounts match an invoice to the purchase order and receipt record?`,
`The match checks that the goods or services were ordered, received and charged on the agreed terms before payment is approved.`],
[8,[3,10],"Pay records, receipts and petty cash",[
`Accounts documents create a traceable record of amounts and authorisation. A payslip identifies the pay period, earnings, deductions and net pay. Gross pay is total earnings before deductions; net pay is gross pay minus the stated deductions. Basic pay may be calculated from hours and an hourly rate; overtime uses the rate and multiplier given. Use authorised time records and the figures in the question rather than assuming a statutory deduction rate.`,
`A receipt identifies the payer, date, amount, purpose, payment method or reference and the authorised recipient or issuing organisation. A payment voucher supports and authorises a payment; it should be linked to the appropriate evidence. Check both words and figures where a document uses both. Preserve the required copy and avoid issuing duplicate acknowledgements that could be mistaken for separate payments.`,
`Petty cash covers authorised small expenses. Under an imprest system, the float is a fixed authorised amount. Record payments using petty cash vouchers and supporting receipts, analyse them into the required expense columns and check cash in hand against the record. Reimbursement normally restores the float by the amount properly spent. An unexplained shortage is a discrepancy to investigate, not an ordinary expense to invent.`
],`A clerk earns $750 per hour for 40 basic hours and works four overtime hours at time-and-a-half. Basic pay is $30,000; overtime is 4 × $750 × 1.5 = $4,500; gross pay is $34,500. Given total deductions of $3,500, net pay is $31,000. Separately, a $5,000 petty cash float with $1,200 properly spent needs a $1,200 reimbursement to return to $5,000.`,
`Do not subtract deductions twice or call gross pay the take-home amount. Replenishing an imprest float is not automatically a new full float in addition to the cash remaining.`,
`A $4,000 imprest float has $850 in authorised vouchers. What cash should remain before reimbursement?`,
`$4,000 − $850 = $3,150 should remain. A reimbursement of $850 restores the authorised float, assuming there is no discrepancy.`],
[8,[5,6,7],"Financial institutions, payments and cheque controls",[
`Financial institutions provide different services. Commercial banks commonly provide deposit, lending and payment services; credit unions are member-based financial cooperatives; building societies and other institutions specialise according to their functions and local arrangements. A central bank has a different role in the financial system from an ordinary customer's retail banking office. Select an institution or service for the actual need, access, cost, terms and security rather than assuming all institutions offer identical products.`,
`Payments may use cash, cheques, payment cards, transfers, direct debits, standing orders or electronic and mobile services. A standing order is an instruction to make scheduled payments under the agreed arrangement; a direct debit authorises collection under its terms. Check the payee, amount, reference, authority and evidence of completion. A transfer request or screenshot should be verified through the approved system before a receipt or release of goods is authorised.`,
`Cheque terminology explains how a cheque is handled. The drawer issues the cheque, the drawee is the institution directed to pay and the payee is the intended recipient. Crossings and account-payee instructions affect collection under applicable practice. Post-dated, stale, dishonoured, stopped, certified or bank-issued cheques need to be distinguished by their purpose and status. Follow current banking rules for an actual payment; never invent a universal validity period. Protect blank stationery and signature authority.`
],`An email asks a clerk to change a supplier's bank details immediately. Verify the request through an independent approved contact and follow the authorisation procedure. Replying to the same unverified email is not an independent check. When receiving a cheque, check the required date, payee, amount and authorisation before processing it.`,
`A cheque is not cash already collected. Do not treat a standing order and direct debit as interchangeable or disclose account credentials to someone claiming to be the bank.`,
`Why should a change to supplier payment details be independently verified?`,
`A fraudulent or mistaken change could divert money. Independent verification and authorisation help confirm that the instruction came from the genuine supplier.`],
[8,[8,9],"Read a bank statement and reconcile the cash book",[
`The cash book records the organisation's cash and bank transactions from its own viewpoint. A bank statement records transactions from the bank's viewpoint. Read the column headings and the effect on the balance rather than assuming that a debit means the same thing in both records. Compare dates, amounts, cheque or transfer references, deposits and charges.`,
`Differences may arise because the bank records items before the organisation does, because recorded items have not yet reached the bank, or because an error occurred. Update the cash book for genuine items not yet entered there, such as bank charges, interest, direct debits, standing orders or a dishonoured cheque. Correct the organisation's errors using the proper procedure. Do not enter an outstanding cheque twice merely because it is absent from the statement.`,
`Then reconcile the adjusted cash book balance with the statement. Starting with a positive bank statement balance, add deposits recorded in the cash book but not yet credited by the bank and subtract cheques issued but not yet presented. Apply the facts carefully if there is an overdraft or a bank error. The reconciliation explains differences; it does not force unrelated numbers to agree. Investigate any remaining unexplained amount.`
],`The statement shows $42,000. A $5,000 deposit is not yet credited, and an issued cheque for $3,000 is not yet presented. The comparable adjusted cash book balance is $42,000 + $5,000 − $3,000 = $44,000. If a $500 bank charge was absent from the cash book, update that record separately before comparing the balances.`,
`Do not adjust the cash book for an outstanding cheque that is already recorded in it. Distinguish a timing difference from a missing entry or an error.`,
`Why does an unpresented cheque make a positive bank statement balance higher than it would otherwise be?`,
`The organisation has already recorded the payment, but the bank has not yet reduced the account for that cheque. Subtract it when reconciling from the statement balance.`],
[9,[1,3],"Procurement duties and responsible purchasing",[
`Procurement obtains the goods and services the organisation needs. Its work includes identifying requirements, obtaining supplier information, comparing quotations, arranging authorised orders, following up delivery and coordinating with receiving, stores and accounts. Inventory management controls the goods held so that operations can continue without unnecessary cost or waste.`,
`A purchasing clerk may prepare enquiries, record quotations, maintain supplier details, prepare orders under authority, track outstanding orders and investigate discrepancies. Accuracy, organisation, communication, integrity and commercial awareness are important. The clerk should protect confidential quotations, disclose conflicts through the appropriate procedure and avoid personal benefits that could distort a purchasing decision.`,
`Separate a request from an approved order. A department can identify a need, but it does not automatically have permission to commit the organisation's funds. Use the approved specifications and authorisation limits. Keep records of what was requested, approved, ordered and received so another officer can follow the transaction and verify payment.`
],`A department requests ten replacement chairs. Purchasing confirms the specifications and approval, obtains suitable quotations and records the authorised choice. If a supplier offers a personal gift to secure the order, the clerk follows the organisation's conflict and gifts procedure rather than letting the offer determine the recommendation.`,
`Procurement is more than buying the cheapest item. A purchase requisition is an internal request; a purchase order is an authorised instruction to a supplier.`,
`Why should supplier quotations be handled impartially?`,
`Impartial handling supports a fair, evidence-based choice and protects the organisation from favouritism, conflicts and poor value.`],
[9,[2,4],"Compare quotations and understand purchasing terms",[
`Compare offers against the required quality, quantity, specification, delivery date, reliability, after-sales service, payment terms and total cost. A quotation states the supplier's offer under specified conditions. Check how long it remains valid and whether delivery, packaging, insurance or other stated charges are included. Goods that arrive too late or fail the specification may cost more overall than a suitable higher-priced offer.`,
`A trade discount reduces a listed price according to the quoted terms; a cash or settlement discount rewards payment within specified conditions. Do not subtract a settlement discount from every invoice regardless of when payment occurs. Credit terms such as 'net 30 days' specify the permitted time for payment. Cash on delivery requires payment under that delivery arrangement.`,
`Abbreviations such as FOB and CIF relate to specified trade terms and responsibilities; read the named place and the terms supplied rather than assuming that every quotation includes the same transport or insurance. E&OE indicates errors and omissions excepted, not permission to ignore checking. In calculations, apply discounts and charges in the order specified and state the currency. Do not combine amounts in different currencies without the given conversion.`
],`Supplier A quotes 20 units at $1,000 each with 10% trade discount and $1,500 delivery. The goods cost $20,000 − $2,000 = $18,000; total quoted cost is $19,500 before any separately stated tax. Compare that with another supplier only after checking that quality, delivery date and included services match.`,
`Do not select a supplier solely from the unit price or confuse a trade discount with a prompt-payment condition. A document abbreviation needs to be read in its transaction context.`,
`How is a 10% trade discount calculated on a $20,000 list total?`,
`The discount is 0.10 × $20,000 = $2,000, leaving $18,000 before any separately stated charges.`],
[9,[5,6],"Follow the purchasing document trail",[
`A typical purchase begins with an internal requisition identifying the need, quantity, specification, required date and requesting department. Purchasing checks authority and obtains suitable supplier information or quotations. After the approved choice, a purchase order states what the supplier is authorised to supply and the agreed terms. Keep a copy and use its reference in follow-up correspondence.`,
`On delivery, the receiving officer checks the goods against the order and delivery documentation, records quantities and condition and reports shortages, damage or substitutions. A goods received note records what the organisation actually received and accepted. Signing a delivery document without checking can make a later discrepancy harder to establish.`,
`Accounts compares the supplier invoice with the order and receipt evidence before approving payment. A credit note can correct or reduce an amount charged, for example after an agreed return or overcharge. A statement of account summarises the balance and transactions over a period; it is not the same as an invoice for a specific supply. Fill in dates, references, descriptions, quantities, prices and totals accurately on each document.`
],`An order requests 30 boxes, but only 28 undamaged boxes arrive. Record 28 accepted, note the two missing and notify the appropriate office and supplier. If the invoice charges for 30, resolve the discrepancy through the approved process rather than changing the physical receipt to match the invoice.`,
`Do not record what was ordered as if it was automatically received. A delivery note is usually supplied with the goods; the goods received note is the receiving organisation's record of its check.`,
`What should be compared before paying a supplier invoice?`,
`Compare the authorised purchase order, evidence of goods or services received and the invoice details, then resolve discrepancies and obtain the required approval.`],
[9,[7,8],"Control inventory and maintain stock records",[
`Inventory is stock held for use or sale. Too little can stop work or cause lost sales; too much ties up funds, uses space and increases the risk of damage, theft or obsolescence. Good inventory management balances availability with cost. Consider usage, delivery lead time, reliability of supply, storage conditions and minimum or reorder levels.`,
`A bin card records quantities received, issued and remaining for one item at its storage location. A stock card or inventory record may also include values and other information. Use authorised receipt and issue documents and record each movement promptly. The running balance equals the previous balance plus receipts minus issues. Identify the item and units clearly: 'boxes' and 'individual units' cannot be mixed without the correct conversion.`,
`Stocktaking compares the physical count with the recorded balance. Investigate differences instead of adjusting a record without explanation. Apply appropriate stock rotation, check expiry or condition where relevant and protect stores against unauthorised access. Manual and electronic systems both need reliable input, controls and trained users. Automatic reorder alerts support the decision; they do not replace the organisation's approval procedure.`
],`An item opens with 40 units. A receipt of 25 raises the balance to 65; an authorised issue of 18 reduces it to 47. If the reorder level is 15, this balance is above it. An attempted issue of 50 should be checked because only 47 units are recorded as available. Use the stock-record tool to see each movement and balance.`,
`Do not count an order placed as stock already received. A reorder level tells the office when to consider ordering; it is not necessarily the quantity to order.`,
`What is the new balance after opening stock of 40, a receipt of 25 and an issue of 18?`,
`40 + 25 − 18 = 47 units. The balance should also be checked against the physical stock under the stocktaking procedure.`],
[10,[1,2,3],"Sales, marketing and customer service work together",[
`Marketing identifies customer needs and helps plan how suitable products or services reach the market. Sales handles enquiries, quotations, orders and the process of obtaining business. Customer service supports customers before, during and after a transaction and helps resolve enquiries and complaints. The functions overlap, but each has a distinct contribution.`,
`A clerk may maintain customer records, prepare correspondence and sales documents, process orders, update information, arrange follow-up and pass enquiries to the right officer. Useful skills include clear communication, product knowledge, accurate record keeping, numeracy, listening, patience, tact and competent use of the approved systems. Reliable information is more useful than an exaggerated promise.`,
`When a customer complains, listen, establish the facts, acknowledge the concern and explain the next appropriate action. Check the order and service record, stay courteous and refer decisions outside your authority. Record the issue and agreed follow-up so the customer does not need to repeat the entire story. Complaint information can also show where the organisation's product or process needs improvement.`
],`A customer reports that a delivery contains the wrong colour. The clerk checks the order reference, the agreed specification and the delivery record, then explains the approved correction process. Blaming the warehouse or promising an unauthorised refund does not resolve the matter professionally.`,
`Customer service is not agreeing to every demand. Good service combines courtesy, accurate information, appropriate authority and reliable follow-up.`,
`Why should a customer complaint be recorded even after the immediate problem is resolved?`,
`The record supports follow-up, consistent handling and investigation of recurring faults that may need a wider process improvement.`],
[10,[4],"Prepare clear sales and marketing documents",[
`A sales enquiry asks for information; a quotation gives an offered price and terms; an order records the customer's request. An invoice lists the goods or services charged and the amount due. A delivery note accompanies a supply, while a receipt acknowledges a payment. A credit note reduces or corrects an amount charged under the agreed circumstances. Read the purpose before deciding which document to prepare.`,
`Check customer and supplier names, dates, item descriptions, quantities, unit prices, discounts, charges, totals, payment terms and references. If a document continues over several lines, make sure the total includes every relevant item once. Write the currency where ambiguity is possible. An attractive document with the wrong customer reference can still cause delay or an incorrect account.`,
`Marketing materials such as notices, brochures, price lists and advertisements should give accurate, relevant information to the intended audience. Use clear headings, readable details and an appropriate contact or response method. Check the authority for prices, claims and publication. Do not add a delivery promise, guarantee or discount that the organisation has not approved.`
],`An invoice contains 12 items at $850 and a delivery charge of $1,000, with no other charges stated. The goods total is $10,200 and the invoice total is $11,200. Before sending it, confirm that the quantity and price match the approved order and that the invoice has a unique reference.`,
`A quotation is not evidence that a sale has already been completed. A receipt should not be issued as if payment was collected merely because an invoice was prepared.`,
`What is the purpose of a credit note issued after an agreed overcharge?`,
`It records the reduction or correction to the amount charged and provides evidence for updating the relevant account.`],
[10,[5,6],"Corporate communication and customer incentives",[
`Corporate communication helps the organisation give accurate, consistent information to its stakeholders. A communications clerk can prepare or distribute authorised notices, maintain contact and media records, monitor enquiries, organise information for events and support internal and external communication. Messages should reflect the organisation's approved position, especially when an issue is sensitive or attracts public attention.`,
`Verify facts, dates, names and authority before publication. Direct a media enquiry to the designated spokesperson rather than guessing an answer. Use professional language, protect confidential information and keep a record of issued material. The same care applies to a public website, social-media post, printed notice or internal bulletin. A quickly published error can damage trust even if the design looks polished.`,
`Incentives encourage customers or sales staff to take a desired action. Customer incentives can include discounts, loyalty benefits, samples, gifts or promotional offers. Sales-force incentives may include commission, bonuses, recognition or other rewards. Compare the purpose, cost, conditions and likely effect. Explain an offer's limits accurately and avoid confusing a temporary promotion with a permanent price or entitlement.`
],`A promotional offer gives a discount only on orders placed before a stated date and above a stated value. The clerk checks those conditions before preparing the price list or responding to an enquiry. If a journalist asks about an unrelated company incident, the clerk records the enquiry and refers it to the authorised spokesperson.`,
`Do not give every customer a discount simply because a promotion exists. Corporate communication does not authorise every clerk to speak publicly on all business matters.`,
`How does a loyalty incentive differ from a sales commission?`,
`A loyalty incentive encourages a customer's repeat business. A commission rewards a salesperson according to the specified sales arrangement.`],
[11,[1,2],"Coordinate operations, dispatch and transport",[
`Operations organises the work needed to produce goods or provide services. Dispatch prepares and sends goods to the correct destination, while transport arranges their movement. These offices coordinate with sales, purchasing, stores, accounts and customers. A correct order that is dispatched to the wrong place has still failed to meet the customer's need.`,
`Clerical duties can include maintaining schedules, preparing movement documents, checking order and delivery details, communicating instructions, recording vehicle or driver information, tracking consignments and following up delays. Accuracy, organisation, communication, safety awareness and the ability to work to deadlines are important. Use the approved procedure for damaged, missing or delayed goods.`,
`Plan dispatch around the correct item, quantity, packaging, destination, delivery time, carrier and required documents. Consider route, capacity, product handling, cost, security and available resources. Urgency should not remove a required check or encourage unsafe transport. Keep authorised people informed when a delay changes the expected delivery.`
],`A food wholesaler has two deliveries with different storage needs. The dispatch clerk checks product handling, quantities, delivery addresses and available transport rather than loading both simply because the vehicle has space. If a delivery cannot meet the agreed time, communicate through the approved channel and record the revised arrangement.`,
`Dispatch is not complete when goods leave the building. The organisation still needs appropriate tracking, acknowledgement and handling of any discrepancy.`,
`Why should dispatch coordinate with sales before changing a delivery arrangement?`,
`Sales may hold the agreement made with the customer. Coordinating prevents conflicting promises and allows the customer to receive accurate information about the change.`],
[11,[3,4],"Complete transport and dispatch documents",[
`Movement documents identify what is being sent, by whom, to whom and under which service. Depending on the transaction, records may include a delivery note, dispatch book, consignment note, waybill, shipping instruction, transport schedule, vehicle log or relevant customs and shipping documents. A bill of lading and an air waybill serve transport-specific functions; use the document and facts given in the question rather than treating every movement record as identical.`,
`Read and enter sender and consignee details, dates, collection and delivery points, package counts, descriptions, weights, references, carrier information and any special handling instructions. The consignee is the intended receiver. Check that quantities and descriptions agree with the authorised order and actual consignment. Do not sign a blank document or acknowledge an unchecked quantity as correct.`,
`Retain the required copies and proof of dispatch or delivery. A vehicle or journey log can record dates, locations, times, distance readings, fuel and other required details. Accurate records support planning, cost control, tracing goods and investigating discrepancies. If an instruction is unclear or a package count differs, resolve it through the appropriate officer before completing the record as if nothing was wrong.`
],`A consignment contains six sealed cartons for Harbour Supplies, but the transport form lists eight. Check the physical count and supporting documents and correct the authorised record before dispatch. On delivery, obtain the required acknowledgement for the actual consignment and retain it with the matching reference.`,
`Do not confuse the consignee with the carrier or assume a dispatch record proves that the goods were received. Read whether a document records instructions, acceptance for transport or completed delivery.`,
`Why should the package count on a consignment note be checked against the goods?`,
`It provides an accurate record of what the carrier accepts and helps identify loss, shortage or a documentation error during transport and delivery.`],

];
